{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":1.0,"text":"In 1943, British spies dressed up a dead man as a fake officer and let him wash up on a Spanish beach, and it fooled Hitler. The Allies wanted to invade Sicily, but everyone expected it, so Britain needed a trick to make the Germans look somewhere else. Intelligence officer Ewen Montagu came up with a wild plan. It was called Operation Mincemeat. First, they needed a body. They found a homeless Welshman who had died after eating rat poison. They gave him a new identity: Major William Martin of the Royal Marines. They even gave him a fiancée, love letters, theater tickets, and a bank overdraft. And in a briefcase chained to his wrist, they put fake top-secret letters hinting that the invasion would hit Greece and Sardinia. On the 30th of April, the submarine HMS Seraph crept up to the coast of Spain, and the crew slid the body into the sea. A Spanish fisherman found him, and the secret papers ended up in German hands. The Germans believed every word. Hitler ordered troops and even General Rommel to Greece and Sardinia. [laughs] On the 10th of July, the Allies landed in Sicily, and the Germans were caught by surprise. The dead man was buried in Spain as William Martin. His real name stayed secret for over 50 years until 1996. Today, his gravestone says he was a Welshman who served as Major William Martin. So if you like history where even a dead man can change a war, follow for more stories like this.","words":[{"text":"In","start":0.04,"end":0.14,"type":"word","logprob":-0.00043072958942502737},{"text":" ","start":0.14,"end":0.18,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.000019192511899746023},{"text":"1943,","start":0.18,"end":1.14,"type":"word","logprob":-0.0003048720536057203},{"text":" ","start":1.14,"end":1.36,"type":"spacing","logprob":-1.9073468138230965e-6},{"text":"British","start":1.36,"end":1.6,"type":"word","logprob":-1.9073468138230965e-6},{"text":" ","start":1.6,"end":1.72,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.00004